// ⚠ KNOWN GAP -- this program prints a WRONG number today. Deliberately NOT wired into nova_ci:
// a gate that asserts current behaviour would lock the bug in. It exists so the defect has an
// executable repro, and it is described in NOVA_DESIGN/WEAPON_PARITY_PLAN.md.
//
// Run it with:  gen3_test.exe build _f31_polyfield_known_gap.nova && _f31_polyfield_known_gap.exe
// Correct output:  poly 1.5 7
// Actual output:   poly 1.5 3.45845952088873e-323      <- the int 7 read as a double
//
// WHAT IS WRONG. Deriving a parameter type from call sites is only valid when EVERY call site is
// visible. Referencing a function by name lowers to a make_closure over a `__fnref_<target>`
// trampoline, and that closure can be invoked from anywhere -- a call the analysis never sees.
// `poly` below is specialized to CPt on the strength of its ONE visible direct call, so
// ir_analyze_return_type labels frt["poly"] float, and the closure path then reads QPt's INT field
// through that float label.
//
// WHY IT IS STILL OPEN. Three fixes were tried and each was a LATERAL move, not an improvement:
//
//   1. Exclude address-taken functions from specialization. Makes the closure call right and the
//      DIRECT call wrong (`poly(p)` printed 4609434218613702656 -- the bits of 1.5 as an integer),
//      because `v.cx` then reads raw double bits into a register labelled `any`.
//   2. Route un-inferrable field reads through nova_rt_field_get at LOWERING time. It boxes using
//      the object's own slot-0 type hash, which is exactly right -- but at lowering the param-type
//      fixpoint has not run yet, so every unannotated struct param still looks un-inferrable and
//      `sdot`-style code fell from fmul/fadd to a runtime name walk.
//   3. Same routing, moved AFTER the fixpoint (correct placement). Still wrong, for a different
//      reason: non-@repr(C) structs are SROA'd onto the stack, and nova_rt_field_get's tag guard
//      only accepts heap structs, so it returned null and `poly(p)` printed 0.
//
// WHAT A REAL FIX NEEDS. The invariant "a float in an `any` context is always boxed" has to hold,
// which spans three places at once: struct float fields are stored as raw doubles; SROA removes the
// runtime tag that the by-name helper depends on; and frt is monotone-refining, so a return type
// once published as float cannot be widened. That is the VALUE_MODEL_OVERHAUL area, not a patch.

type CPt
    cx: float
    cy: float

type QPt
    cx: int
    cy: int

fn poly(v)
    v.cx

fn apply_it(f, v)
    f(v)

fn main()
    let p = CPt { cx: 1.5, cy: 2.5 }
    let r = QPt { cx: 7, cy: 8 }
    // want "poly 1.5 7"
    print("poly " + str(poly(p)) + " " + str(apply_it(poly, r)))
